#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:50
msgid "Sensitivities and data fitting using PyBaMM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:52
msgid ""
"PyBaMM input parameters ```pybamm.InputParameter`` <https://docs.pybamm.org/"
"en/stable/source/api/expression_tree/input_parameter.html>`__ can be used to "
"run many simulations with varying parameters. Here we will demonstrate "
"PyBaMM's ability to calculate the senstivities of model outputs with respect "
"to input parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:54
msgid ""
"To be more specific, given a model output :math:`f(a)`, where :math:`a` is "
"an input parameter, we wish to calculate :math:`\\frac{\\partial f}"
"{\\partial a}(a)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:56
msgid "First we will demonstrate using a toy model, given by the equations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:58
msgid ""
"\\frac{dy}{dt} = a y\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:60
msgid ""
"with a scalar state variable :math:`y` and a scalar parameter :math:`a`, and "
"initial conditions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:62
msgid ""
"y(0) = 1\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:64
msgid "We will also define a model output given by :math:`f = y^2`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid "[2]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# setup a simple test model\n"
"model = pybamm.BaseModel(\"name\")\n"
"y = pybamm.Variable(\"y\")\n"
"a = pybamm.InputParameter(\"a\")\n"
"model.rhs = {y: a * y}\n"
"model.initial_conditions = {y: 1}\n"
"model.variables = {\"y squared\": y**2}\n"
"\n"
"solver = pybamm.IDAKLUSolver(rtol=1e-10, atol=1e-10)\n"
"t_eval = np.linspace(0, 1, 80)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:94
msgid ""
"Note that we have used the ```pybamm.IDAKLUSolver`` <https://docs.pybamm.org/"
"en/stable/source/api/solvers/idaklu_solver.html>`__ solver for this example, "
"this is currently the recommended solver for calculating sensitivities in "
"PyBaMM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:96
msgid ""
"We can solve the model using a specific value of :math:`a = 1`. However, now "
"we will also calculate the forward sensitivities of the model by setting the "
"argument ``calculate_sensitivies=True``. Note that this argument will also "
"accept a list of input parameters to calculate the sensitivities for, but "
"setting it to ``True`` will calculate the sensitivities for **all** input "
"parameters of the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid "[3]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"solution = solver.solve(model, [0, 1], inputs={\"a\": 1}, "
"calculate_sensitivities=True)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:117
msgid ""
"We can now access the solution as normal, and the sensitivities using the "
"syntax: ``solution[output_name].sensitivities[input_parameter_name]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:119
msgid ""
"Note that a current restriction to the sensitivity calculation is that it "
"will only return the sensitivities at the values of ``t_eval`` used to solve "
"the model. Any interpolation between these values will have to be done "
"manually"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid "[4]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"fig, axs = plt.subplots(1, 2)\n"
"axs[0].plot(t_eval, solution[\"y squared\"](t_eval))\n"
"axs[0].set_ylabel(r\"$y^2$\")\n"
"axs[0].set_xlabel(r\"$t$\")\n"
"axs[1].plot(solution.t, solution[\"y squared\"].sensitivities[\"a\"])\n"
"axs[1].set_ylabel(r\"$\\frac{dy^2}{da}$\")\n"
"axs[1].set_xlabel(r\"$t$\")\n"
"plt.tight_layout()\n"
"plt.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:157
msgid "Sensitivities for the DFN model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:159
msgid ""
"We can do the same for the DFN model included in PyBaMM. We will setup the "
"DFN model using \"Current function\" as an input parameter. This is the "
"parameter we wish to calculate the sensitivities with respect to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid "[5]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# now lets do the same for the DFN model\n"
"\n"
"# load model\n"
"model = pybamm.lithium_ion.DFN()\n"
"\n"
"# load parameter values and process model and geometry\n"
"param = model.default_parameter_values\n"
"\n"
"# we want to calculate the sensitivities of the \"Current function\" "
"parameter, so set\n"
"# this an an input parameter\n"
"param.update({\"Current function [A]\": \"[input]\"})\n"
"\n"
"solver = pybamm.IDAKLUSolver(rtol=1e-3, atol=1e-6)\n"
"\n"
"sim = pybamm.Simulation(model, parameter_values=param, solver=solver)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:194
msgid ""
"We can now evaluate the senstivities of, for example, the \"Terminal "
"voltage\" output of the model with respect to the input parameter \"Current "
"function\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid "[6]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"solution = sim.solve(\n"
"    [0, 3600], inputs={\"Current function [A]\": 0.15652}, "
"calculate_sensitivities=True\n"
")\n"
"plt.plot(\n"
"    solution.t, solution[\"Terminal voltage [V]\"].sensitivities[\"Current "
"function [A]\"]\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"plt.xlabel(r\"$t$\")\n"
"plt.ylabel(\"sensitivities of Terminal voltage wrt Current fuction\")\n"
"plt.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:233
msgid "Sensitivities and data fitting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:235
msgid ""
"Sensitivities are often used to aid data fitting by providing a means to "
"calculate the gradient of the function to be minimised. Take for example the "
"data fitting exercise we introduced in the previous notebook. Once again we "
"will generate some fake data for fitting, like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid "[7]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"t_eval = np.linspace(0, 3600, 100)\n"
"data = sim.solve([0, 3600], inputs={\"Current function [A]\": 0.2222})[\n"
"    \"Terminal voltage [V]\"\n"
"](t_eval)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:259
msgid ""
"Now we will contruct a function to minimise, but here we will return both "
"the value of the function, and its gradient with respect to the input "
"parameter \"Current function\". Note that our objective function is the sum "
"of squared different between the vector :math:`\\mathbf{f}`, the simulated "
"\"Terminal voltage\", and :math:`\\mathbf{d}`, the vector of fake data, "
"given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:261
msgid ""
"\\mathcal{O}(a) = \\sum_{i=0}^{i=N} (f_i(a) - d_i)^2\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:263
msgid ""
"where :math:`a` is the parameter to be optimised (in this case \"Current "
"function\"), :math:`f_i` is each element of the vector :math:`\\mathbf{f}`, "
"and :math:`d_i` is each element of :math:`\\mathbf{d}`. We wish to also find "
"the gradient of this function wrt the parameter :math:`a`, which is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:265
msgid ""
"\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{O}}{\\partial a}(a) = 2 \\sum_{i=0}^{i=N} (f_i(a) "
"- d_i) \\frac{\\partial f_i}{\\partial a}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:267
msgid ""
"Using these equations, we will define a function that takes in as an "
"argument :math:`a`, and returns :math:`(\\mathcal{O}(a), \\frac{\\partial "
"\\mathcal{O}}{\\partial a}(a))`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid "[8]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"def sum_of_squares_jac(parameters):\n"
"    sol = sim.solve(\n"
"        [0, 3600],\n"
"        t_interp=t_eval,\n"
"        inputs={\"Current function [A]\": parameters[0]},\n"
"        calculate_sensitivities=True,\n"
"    )\n"
"    term_voltage = sol[\"Terminal voltage [V]\"].data\n"
"    term_voltage_sens = sol[\"Terminal voltage [V]\"].sensitivities[\n"
"        \"Current function [A]\"\n"
"    ]\n"
"\n"
"    f = np.sum((term_voltage - data) ** 2)\n"
"    g = 2 * np.sum((term_voltage - data) * term_voltage_sens)\n"
"    print(\n"
"        f\"evaluating function and jacobian for p = {parameters[0]}, \\tloss "
"= {f}, grad = {g}\"\n"
"    )\n"
"    return f, g"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:305
msgid ""
"We can then use this function along with an optimisation algorithm to "
"recover the value of the Current function that was used to generate the "
"data. In this case we will use the ``scipy.optimize`` module again. This "
"module allows the use of a function in the form given above to perform the "
"minimisation, using both the value of the objective function and its "
"gradient to find the minimum value of :math:`a` in the least number of steps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:307
msgid ""
"Once again, we will place bounds on \"Current function [A]\" between :math:"
"`(0.01, 0.6)`, and use a random starting value :math:`x_0` between these "
"bounds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid "[9]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/parameterization/sensitivities_and_data_fitting.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"bounds = (0.01, 0.6)\n"
"x0 = np.random.uniform(low=bounds[0], high=bounds[1])\n"
"\n"
"print(\"starting parameter is\", x0)\n"
"res = scipy.optimize.minimize(sum_of_squares_jac, [x0], bounds=[bounds], "
"jac=True)\n"
"print(\"recovered parameter is\", res.x[0])"
msgstr ""

